ing. It’s not predicting. It’s scheduling.”
    The room was quiet.
    “They find the kids who push back,” I said. “They flag them. They
offer programs, a stipend, a laptop. They hum the loop at them for the
next eighteen years. And then they write down what happened, and
say, look, we were right.”
    “That’s the research,” said Sofía. “That’s what the foundation is for.”
    She turned to the last page, an appendix nobody had bothered to
format. One chart: incidents per block per week, every spike followed
by a long flat trough. The label on the trough said Post-event quiescence
(yield).
    “Yield,” said Sofía. “They measure how quiet a block gets after it
fights. That’s the number they’re proud of.”
    I’d seen that word in brown ink on a page from 1838. Rendimiento.
    I went out to the sidewalk, because the café had gone too quiet to
think in, and found the photo I’d taken of Abuela’s airmail envelope. N.
